WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:04.041
When experts disagree, you can't judge the content,

2
00:00:04.157 --> 00:00:05.840
but you can structure the decision.

3
00:00:06.922 --> 00:00:11.787
Mostly it is not about facts, but about different assumptions or priorities.

4
00:00:12.824 --> 00:00:16.981
Step one: the decision criteria on the table, before the options.

5
00:00:17.685 --> 00:00:20.204
Cost, risk, time, quality, upkeep.

6
00:00:21.208 --> 00:00:22.880
Separate fact from judgement.

7
00:00:23.602 --> 00:00:26.621
The transformer is 42 years old: a fact.

8
00:00:27.368 --> 00:00:29.318
Ten more years: an assessment.

9
00:00:30.399 --> 00:00:33.162
Disagreements usually sit in the judgements.

10
00:00:34.172 --> 00:00:36.982
Ask each expert: what would change your mind?

11
00:00:37.812 --> 00:00:39.867
Then you see what it really rests on.

12
00:00:41.222 --> 00:00:45.610
A weighted score: a weight for each criterion, a score for each option.

13
00:00:46.641 --> 00:00:48.266
Multiply and add up.

14
00:00:48.954 --> 00:00:49.976
It's not magic.

15
00:00:51.043 --> 00:00:55.304
The weights are a business choice, not a technical one: set them with the sponsor.

16
00:00:56.333 --> 00:00:59.096
The real value: you see where the options differ,

17
00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:03.263
and whether the outcome changes if a weight moves a little.

18
00:01:04.636 --> 00:01:07.829
A second opinion: an independent outside expert,

19
00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.279
for big choices that are hard to undo.

20
00:01:11.259 --> 00:01:15.183
Brief them on the question and the criteria, not on who said what.

21
00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:20.956
Also useful: have each state the other's position until the other agrees.

22
00:01:21.686 --> 00:01:25.123
It cools things down; often they are closer than they sounded.

23
00:01:26.144 --> 00:01:30.428
Typical failures: deciding by seniority, by who talks loudest,

24
00:01:30.614 --> 00:01:34.097
or splitting the difference into a solution nobody believes in.

25
00:01:35.109 --> 00:01:38.046
Or letting it drag on, while the project waits.

26
00:01:39.086 --> 00:01:43.138
A sharp question: if we chose your colleague's option, what would go wrong,

27
00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:44.194
and how likely?

28
00:01:44.911 --> 00:01:46.409
Opinions become risks.

29
00:01:47.731 --> 00:01:49.716
A grid project at a factory.

30
00:01:50.397 --> 00:01:54.402
Its own electrical engineer wants to service the old transformer,

31
00:01:54.565 --> 00:01:56.307
the installer to replace it.

32
00:01:57.393 --> 00:02:00.493
Step one, with the sponsor: cost over 10 years,

33
00:02:00.691 --> 00:02:03.118
outage risk, downtime during the work.

34
00:02:04.142 --> 00:02:04.873
Step two.

35
00:02:05.571 --> 00:02:08.590
Fact: the oil test shows rising moisture.

36
00:02:09.305 --> 00:02:12.847
Judgement: 10 more years, versus failure within 5.

37
00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:16.027
Step three: what would change your mind?

38
00:02:16.777 --> 00:02:19.563
The factory: a worse oil test next year.

39
00:02:20.291 --> 00:02:22.300
The installer: a clean test.

40
00:02:23.373 --> 00:02:28.319
Step four: a second opinion from an independent test company on the oil results.

41
00:02:29.318 --> 00:02:31.780
It supports replacing within 3 years;

42
00:02:31.966 --> 00:02:34.578
the sponsor plans it for next summer's shutdown.

43
00:02:35.935 --> 00:02:40.161
Experts are usually the scarcest resource: few people can do the work,

44
00:02:40.335 --> 00:02:41.705
many projects want them.

45
00:02:42.703 --> 00:02:45.664
Your plan is only as real as their availability.

46
00:02:46.626 --> 00:02:51.398
Context switching: moving between tasks, and losing time getting back in.

47
00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:57.544
Gerald Weinberg's rule of thumb, 1992: on two projects,

48
00:02:57.683 --> 00:02:59.634
about a fifth goes on switching.

49
00:03:00.629 --> 00:03:03.590
On five, the loss can approach three quarters.

50
00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:06.468
A rough guide, not a measurement.

51
00:03:07.816 --> 00:03:12.877
Ask for blocks, not percentages: Tuesdays and Thursdays beats 40 percent.

52
00:03:13.929 --> 00:03:17.203
Prepare, so their hours count: questions written down,

53
00:03:17.342 --> 00:03:19.432
data ready, decisions lined up.

54
00:03:20.464 --> 00:03:21.637
Not needed in a meeting?

55
00:03:22.334 --> 00:03:24.447
Send a two-line summary instead.

56
00:03:25.485 --> 00:03:29.595
A timebox is a fixed slot for a task that ends when the time is up.

57
00:03:30.601 --> 00:03:35.036
For example: two hours on Thursday to decide the interface design.

58
00:03:36.322 --> 00:03:41.883
Daniel Pink, in Drive, from 2009, names three drivers for knowledge work,

59
00:03:42.046 --> 00:03:46.446
building on the self-determination theory of Edward Deci and Richard Ryan.

60
00:03:47.466 --> 00:03:50.125
Autonomy: control over how you do the work.

61
00:03:51.207 --> 00:03:54.226
Mastery: getting better at something that matters to you.

62
00:03:55.233 --> 00:03:57.358
Purpose: seeing what the work is for.

63
00:03:58.395 --> 00:04:01.774
So: autonomy over the how, and firm on the what and when.

64
00:04:02.753 --> 00:04:03.635
Tell them the problem,

65
00:04:03.809 --> 00:04:08.186
not just the task: the operators lose 20 minutes a shift on this.

66
00:04:09.195 --> 00:04:12.178
Credit their work by name, in front of the sponsor.

67
00:04:13.246 --> 00:04:17.251
Motivation dies with priorities changed without explanation,

68
00:04:17.379 --> 00:04:21.849
meetings without purpose, and advice overruled without being told why.

69
00:04:23.135 --> 00:04:27.256
A typical failure: treating the expert's manager as the only lever.

70
00:04:28.345 --> 00:04:31.561
Their manager allocates hours; you earn their attention.

71
00:04:32.566 --> 00:04:35.910
Ask at the start: what would make this project worth your time?

72
00:04:36.951 --> 00:04:41.572
An interesting problem, a clean handover, or simply not being called at night.

73
00:04:42.681 --> 00:04:46.048
Then remember the answer: that is how you earn their attention.

74
00:04:47.373 --> 00:04:48.255
A robot cell.

75
00:04:48.968 --> 00:04:52.555
The supplier's only safety specialist is on four projects,

76
00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:54.541
and keeps missing your sessions.

77
00:04:55.545 --> 00:05:00.735
In 8 weeks you need some 40 hours of him; his manager says 25 percent.

78
00:05:01.786 --> 00:05:03.887
On paper, that is 80 hours.

79
00:05:04.915 --> 00:05:10.105
But Weinberg's table gives about 10 percent each on four projects: 32 hours,

80
00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:10.824
too few.

81
00:05:11.839 --> 00:05:17.365
So ask his manager for blocks: one fixed 8-hour day every two weeks: 32 hours.

82
00:05:18.398 --> 00:05:23.937
Plus two half days for the safety test, booked now: 40 hours of concentrated time.

83
00:05:24.951 --> 00:05:27.818
Prepare each day, so no hour is wasted.

84
00:05:28.883 --> 00:05:33.155
Ask what makes it worth his while, perhaps fewer late calls from the site.

85
00:05:33.907 --> 00:05:36.043
Credit his work in the steering meeting.
